-- title:
--   Correspondence 7.17 — the $r$-local nested set is the global one
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a locally finite, connected graph whose binary cycle space is generated by cycles of length at most $r$, let $\mathcal B$ be a set of bottlenecks in $G$, and let $\mathcal B_r=\{\beta_r:\beta\in\mathcal B\}$. Then for every $k\in\mathbb N$:
--
--   1. $N^k_r(G)$ (Construction 7.16 for $\mathcal B_r$) is well-defined: all crossing numbers $x^k_r(s)$ are finite;
--   2. $N^k_r(G)=\{s_r:s\in N^k(G)\}$, where $N^k(G)$ is given by Construction 3.6 for $\mathcal B$;
--   3. in particular $N^k_r(G)$ is nested;
--   4. if $\mathcal B_r$ is $\Gamma$-invariant for a subgroup $\Gamma\le\operatorname{Aut}(G)$, then $N^k_r(G)$ is $\Gamma$-canonical.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: the binary cycle space of $G$ is generated by cycles of length $\le r$ → every cycle's edge set is a finite symmetric difference of edge sets of cycles of length $\le r$ (`CycleSpaceGenByShort`); reason: the finite cycle space; the proofs (Lemma 4.3, Correspondence 4.4) argue with finite $\mathbb Z/2$ sums
--   - reading: the sets $N^k$ are well-defined → every crossing number $x_k(s)$ on which they depend is finite; reason: Construction 3.6/7.16 counts crossings 'assuming for now that this number is finite'; the count is a cardinal in $\mathbb N_\infty$
--   - reading: $s \succ t$ (used without a separate definition) and crossing/$\succcurlyeq$ defined for $r$-local separations with $r$-tomic separators → $s \succ t$ is $s \succcurlyeq t \wedge s \ne t$; the crossing and $\succcurlyeq$ conditions are defined for all pairs; reason: strict part of $\succcurlyeq$, as $>$ for $\geqslant$ in §8.1; the statement only applies them to separations with $r$-tomic separators
--   - encoding: orders $|X|$ of separators → `Set.encard` in $\mathbb N_\infty$; reason: separators of the infinite cover can be infinite (p.29); no finite instance changes
--   - encoding: $G$ locally finite → every neighbour set is finite (a `Prop`, not Mathlib's data-carrying `LocallyFinite` class); reason: same graphs
-- source:
--   Jacobs, Knappe, Kurkofka, Canonical Graph Decompositions via Local Separations, preprint (STOC 2027 submission, arXiv:2501.16170; sha256 9a11d48d…), Correspondence 7.17, pp.46–47

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LocalSepDecomp_Cutouts

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace LocalSepDecomp

open SimpleGraph

universe u

/-- **Correspondence 7.17** (Nested set). R. W. Jacobs, P. Knappe, J. Kurkofka, *Canonical Graph Decompositions via Local Separations*,
preprint (STOC 2027 submission, arXiv:2501.16170; sha256 9a11d48d…), Correspondence 7.17, pp.46–47.
Let `G` be a locally finite, connected graph whose binary cycle space is generated by cycles of
length `≤ r`, let `B` be a set of bottlenecks in `G` and `B_r = {β_r : β ∈ B}`. Then for every `k`
the set `N^k_r(G)` (Construction 7.16 for `B_r`) is well-defined (all crossing numbers
`x^k_r(s)` are finite) and equals `{s_r : s ∈ N^k(G)}` (Construction 3.6 for `B`); in particular it
is nested; and if `B_r` is `Γ`-invariant, then `N^k_r(G)` is `Γ`-canonical. -/
theorem correspondence_7_17 {V : Type u} (G : SimpleGraph V) (r : ℕ) (hlf : IsLocallyFinite G)
    (hG : G.Connected) (B : Set (Set (Sym2 (Set V)))) (hB : ∀ β ∈ B, ∃ k, IsBottleneck G k β)
    (hcyc : CycleSpaceGenByShort G r) (k : ℕ) :
    (∀ s ∈ ⋃₀ localBk G r (bottleneckToLocal G '' B) k,
        lxk G r (bottleneckToLocal G '' B) k s < ⊤) ∧
      LNk G r (bottleneckToLocal G '' B) k = usepToULSep G '' Nk G B k ∧
      ULSetNested G r (LNk G r (bottleneckToLocal G '' B) k) ∧
      ∀ Γ : Subgroup (G ≃g G), IsInvariantLBottleneckSet G Γ (bottleneckToLocal G '' B) →
        IsCanonicalULSepSet G Γ (LNk G r (bottleneckToLocal G '' B) k) := by sorry

end LocalSepDecomp
